[Music]  meat is choking our planet  if you care about climate change and you  have red meat on your plate you are not  taking this seriously nearly 15 percent  of greenhouse gas emissions come from  crop and livestock production meat is  incredibly environmentally intensive all  the land all of the water all the carbon  intensity what people put on their  plates today impacts the future of the  planet sustainability will come into my  head  but i can't really get my taste buds  around like the mushiness of corn i just  fall back onto meat  even as meat consumption around the  world increases people are being offered  more and more choices to make their  diets more sustainable but can meat  alternatives really lead to a more  sustainable world  [Music]  28 year old jay taylor has been  kickboxing for nine years  i had my first proper fight in 2014.  i'm just trying to push on from there  and progress as much as i can now  eight weeks before a fight jay enters a  strict training regime a vital part of  that is his diet protein for my diet is  fundamental  i need to make sure i'm hitting the  right amount keeping my muscle mass as  high as i can  and healing  protein helps you heal  [Music]  wow  you get me an egg out please yeah jay's  girlfriend lydia has been vegetarian for  two years okay thank you  i found at uni that i ate less meat  because of affordability and it being  better for the environment  lydia would like jay to follow suit i  like to talk to joe about phoenix just  to make him aware that  i mean everyone knows that meat isn't  the best for the environment it's the  tastiest  it's not though if you actually had an  open mind when it came to your palette  we can have dinner i look at your stuff  and i see mine and i'm like  nah stick with that  jay is not the only one in a british  survey 76 percent of people said they  cared about the environment but just 26  would stop eating meat and other animal  products to reduce their environmental  impact  two-thirds of people said they would  rather die five to ten years younger  than give up meat  and that's a problem  because we humans eat a lot of meat  wow sharon's 90th birthday she thought  it goes to rain  from the british meat marketing board to  american fast food chains the public's  been fed juicy cuts for decades  327 million tons of it globally each  year  how about a little british beef british  beef makes everyone feel at home  the idea behind the project is that  we're investigating the impact of eating  meat and dairy on the environment leap  is a four-year research project  analyzing global food systems i've  learned that eating beef has a much  bigger environmental impact than eating  chicken  the man behind it is oxford university  professor sir charles godfrey one way of  thinking about this is to do a thought  experiment let's suppose we cure the  greenhouse gas emissions from all of the  economy apart from food so we solved  heating we solve transport but we  haven't sold food if we continue  business as usual with the food system  into the future  emissions from the food system alone are  sufficient to push us almost certainly  over 1.5 and probably over 2 degrees  warming and two degrees of warming  that's about the maximum that we could  possibly have without really disruptive  climate change  so if we care about climate change we  really do have to think about the food  system the food system as a whole is  responsible for about a third of the  world's greenhouse gas emissions  almost half of that comes from crop and  livestock production  if everyone was to switch to a  plant-based diet then our greenhouse gas  emissions would go down quite  substantially  the earth is dying we are not saying  this 14 000 scientists across the world  are unequivocal evidence of climate  change as climate change gets pushed  higher and higher up the political  agenda more and more companies coming up  with alternative products that attempt  to fill the desire for meat  and here in california is one that puts  tackling climate change at the heart of  its mission  market leader impossible foods is  helping to transform a sector which was  valued at 29.4 billion dollars in 2020  but is forecast to reach 162 billion  dollars by the end of the decade  [Music]  the company was founded to really  effectively address two big global  crises the first big global crisis we're  all talking about right now it's it's  climate change the second issue that is  not necessarily getting quite as much  airtime but is very much related is the  extinction crisis  so that's just loss of biodiversity it's  loss of wildlife and that is something  that is very much linked to climate  change but is potentially even more of  an urgent issue  to help it deliver on its mission the  company asked a sustainability  consultancy to measure the overall  environmental impact of every one of  their products and compare it to the  animal equivalent  for the impossible burger it's about 89  percent less greenhouse gases it's about  87 percent less water and we need about  96 less land and you know we've found  them to actually be pretty effective in  terms of a consumer engagement tactic so  there's a lot of interest at the moment  in the environmental effects of  alternative protein now we can  say with confidence that  those type of alternative proteins which  are now operating at scale for example  producing plant-based proteins then they  tend to have a lower environmental  impact than producing meat in the  traditional way  greenhouse gases could be reduced by as  much as 70 percent if everyone was to  switch to a vegan diet  but that really does seem impossible  just five percent of americans identify  as vegetarian and only two percent is  vegan numbers that have held steady for  years  so to really make an impact on the  environment it's going to need an  alternative that's palatable to the  millions who are determined not to swap  their meat for plants  can you do the honours and lift the lid  on your creation  i can  there's quite some intense taste it's  close to meat in 2013 the first  lab-grown or cultured meat burger was  created it cost a whopping 330  000 to produce  fast forward to december 2020 a  californian firm eat just becomes the  first to get regulatory approval in  singapore for its cultured meat  so we start with a cell  and we get a cell from a biopsy a cell  bank a fresh piece of meat then we  identify nutrients to feed the cell so  think about the soy and corn that goes  in the chicken's body and then the  muscle and the fat in the body ends up  growing and then instead of having the  animal's body in a warehouse growing we  grow our meat in a large stainless steel  vessel kind of like brewing beer called  a bioreactor and the end product is what  you're used to  it's real meat it tastes like chicken  because it is chicken  producing meat and steel vessels in  warehouses dotted around towns and  cities could free up vast amounts of  land it would allow countries that  currently import most of their meat to  simply grow it themselves  and of course there could be more direct  environmental benefits too with this  approach you don't need to tear down  trees it's less carbon intensive use  less water and significantly less land  so both from a carbon a land a water  perspective orders a magnitude better  you can build facilities really anywhere  these are not facilities that require  tens of millions of acres you can make  it pretty close to home  recent studies looking at the potential  environmental benefits of cultured meat  production found the global warming  impact could be reduced by as much as 92  percent but in reality it's too soon to  tell this is an industry that's still in  its infancy  because that has yet to be taken to  scale it's very hard to estimate what  the environmental effects are going to  be at the moment they tend to be quite  high but that's because they're done  in laboratories using a lot of  electricity in principle and certainly  people in the field argue there is a  potential for it to be considerably less  with cultured meat yet to hit the mass  market and plant-based alternatives  seemingly still an acquired taste it's  clear that for now it'll take more than  consumer choice to help deliver a more  sustainable world  you've got to do what's right for the  planet and to get that change you're  going to need a persuasive alternative  currently the alternatives out there  aren't that persuasive for me  and whatever the potential benefits the  food industry as a whole is unlikely to  turn green unless it's led there  i think that sort of more relevant for  policy is that  were there to be a shift towards more  plant-based diets that would have very  positive effects for the environment but  i would say that arguments like that  very much worried livestock producers  completely understandably their  livelihoods depend on producing meat so  i think it's very important to think how  we can transition from our current diets  to diets with  less meat in it but that also is a just  transition for the people that are  involved in the system  the plant-based meat industry the food  systems  sector as a whole hasn't really had at  any level  the level of policy engagement that the  transportation sector has the energy  sector has even the built environment  sector has  if you look at the improvements that  have been made in how we source energy i  think we can attribute a lot of that to  policy interventions for the time being  though innovative businesses pushing  meat alternatives may have to settle for  saving the planet one burger at a time  thanks very much for watching you can  read more about the future of food and  my technology quarterly by clicking the  link and please don't forget to  subscribe  [Music]

